```java
class SafeCache<K, V> {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, V> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    /** Atomically inserts value if key is absent; returns the winning value. */
    V putIfAbsent(K key, V value) {
        return map.putIfAbsent(key, value);
    }
}
```

This is Java, but the pattern is general: instead of exposing `containsKey` and `put` separately and hoping callers never combine them unsafely, expose the *compound operation itself* as one atomic method, and document exactly what it guarantees in its comment -- here, "atomically inserts if absent."

`ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent` implements this internally without a full lock, using finer-grained synchronization per bucket -- but the caller doesn't need to know that. The caller only needs the documented contract: one atomic check-and-insert, full stop.
